Package: mos-system
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: systemd, systemd-sysv, systemd-resolved, systemd-repart, systemd-timesyncd, udev, dbus, kmod, openssh-server, iproute2, nftables, iptables, libubootenv-tool, curl, passwd
Description: mos common system policy, state mounts and operator account
 The board-independent half of a mos root filesystem: the seed and reconcile
 tools under /usr/lib/mos, their units, the STATE and DATA bind mounts
 (/var/lib/mos, /mos, /srv, /etc/ssh, /etc/hostname, /home, /root,
 /usr/local/lib/systemd/system), the six board-independent repart.d position
 holders, the /var fill-up policy, the image's default DHCP network and
 journald's volatile storage policy.
 .
 It owns the /var/lib/mos mountpoint. That directory and var-lib-mos.mount are
 two halves of one mechanism -- mosd.service declares
 RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/mos -- so mosd names this package in Depends
 rather than shipping the directory itself.
 .
 It ships the preset that keeps sshd disabled on both profiles, because mosd
 owns that lifecycle: openssh-server's own postinst would otherwise enable
 ssh.service, and openssh-server is in Depends above partly so that dpkg unpacks
 this preset before it is configured. The postinst asserts that outcome; it does
 not arrange it.
 .
 The postinst creates the pinned mos operator account, uid and gid 1000, home
 /home/mos, shell /bin/bash. The uid is written out rather than allocated
 because /home/mos lives on the DATA partition and survives every A/B update,
 so its owner is part of the on-disk contract. The account is granted no
 supplementary groups and no sudo, and it is not a lesser privilege level:
 sshd authorises root and mos from one key list.
 .
 Architecture: all, and the Depends above asks for no shlibs substitution.
 Nothing in the payload is an ELF, so there is no shared-library dependency
 for the packer to resolve -- asking would be an error rather than an empty
 expansion. Every entry above is a relationship ELF metadata could not show
 anyway: systemd-repart is a separate package on trixie and its absence is
 silent -- DATA simply never grows; libubootenv-tool provides fw_printenv for
 the machine-id oneshot; curl is what the health gate probes apid with, and
 without it that probe SKIPs and the gate reports green over two of three
 components; passwd provides the useradd, groupadd and chage the postinst
 execs by name.
 .
 systemd-timesyncd is here because network time is base policy on every image
 (PLAN-044), and this package ships the two files that govern it: the pinned
 policy drop-in under /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf.d and the
 sysinit.target.wants enablement link. The daemon those files configure and
 enable has to arrive with them -- a root holding the link without the unit is
 a service systemd cannot start and a wants-symlink that resolves to nothing.
 Unversioned, like systemd above: an upstream Debian package, not a pool
 member with a stamp to pin.
 .
 THESE ARE TOOLS AND NOT A FIREWALL. nftables and iptables are in Depends beside
 iproute2 and curl for the same reason those are: base tooling every image
 carries whatever it declines. This package ships no rule, no policy and nothing
 that would persist one. Before they were added, the only firewall front-end in
 any mos image was nft, and it arrives through mos-podman's Depends -- netavark
 execs it off PATH -- so a board or a build that declined containers had NO way
 to read or write a netfilter rule at all.
 .
 TWO FRONT-ENDS, ONE BACKEND, AND THEY DO NOT SHOW EACH OTHER THE SAME THING.
 Both program the kernel's nf_tables subsystem: on trixie the iptables name
 resolves through alternatives to iptables-nft, a translation layer and not a
 second firewall. But `nft list ruleset` shows the WHOLE subsystem -- rules
 written through either tool, and the tables netavark reconciles -- while
 `iptables -S` shows only what came through the iptables front-end. nft is the
 complete view and the one to reach for; iptables is here so that third-party
 tooling and operator habits that cannot speak nft keep working. The legacy
 xtables binaries ship in the same Debian package as iptables and nothing here
 selects them: the alternatives group is left in auto mode, nft outranks legacy
 20 to 10, and update-alternatives is run nowhere in this tree.
 .
 nftables IS ALSO IN mos-podman's Depends AND THAT IS NOT A DUPLICATION. A
 package that needs a tool declares it: mos-podman needs nft because netavark
 runs it off PATH, and this package carries it because the base image's firewall
 vocabulary is nft whether or not containers are present. Those are two
 independent true statements, and the contents of the base package are not
 something mos-podman may assume.
 .
 THE PRESET AT /usr/lib/systemd/system-preset/50-mos-nftables.preset IS WHY
 nftables IS SAFE TO DECLARE HERE, and it is a decision rather than a tidy-up.
 The nftables package ships nftables.service, whose ExecStart is
 `nft -f /etc/nftables.conf`, and that shipped config begins with `flush
 ruleset` -- an enabled unit would clear netavark's container-network rules at
 every boot. No shipped preset matched that unit before this one, and MEASURED
 in a clean trixie root, `systemctl preset nftables.service` with the stock set
 CREATES sysinit.target.wants/nftables.service: the unmatched fallback is
 enable. With `disable nftables.service` in place the same command writes
 nothing. The unit being unenabled today is an absence; this file makes it a
 decision the tree owns, and it is what a `systemctl preset-all` or a future
 upstream packaging change would otherwise walk straight through.
 .
 THE COST, measured on the composed x64 dev root: 450035 KiB over 208 packages
 before either dependency, 452804 KiB over 214 after -- 2768 KiB of closure and
 one more KiB for the preset file above, which is this package's own payload and
 not a dependency's. An image WITH containers
 pays six packages and 2768 KiB, all of it iptables' -- iptables, libip4tc2,
 libip6tc2, libnetfilter-conntrack3, libnfnetlink0 and netbase, the last for
 /etc/protocols and /etc/services -- because nftables, libnftables1, libjansson4
 and libnftnl11 were already there through mos-podman. An image that DECLINES
 containers pays ten packages and 4351 KiB, the same six plus nftables 185,
 libnftables1 1054, libjansson4 100 and libnftnl11 244; that profile is the one
 these two dependencies actually change. libmnl is in neither list: iproute2
 already depends on it.
 .
 RAUC's runtime libraries are deliberately not here. glib, json-glib and
 libfdisk are in the image for the self-built rauc alone and belong to
 mos-rauc; they are installed by the base stage today only because the stage
 chain had nowhere else to put them.
